    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Hey Kylie</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">I noticed something odd, while I analyzed my data with\r\nCardinal. The images have the wrong orientation. They are flip upside-down compared\r\nto the optical image and compared to ion images generated with Biomap or\r\nMSIReader for example.</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">My data comes from an AB Sciex 5800 and is in the Analyze\r\n7.5 data format.</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Attached are two images; an optical one and one from\r\nCardinal.</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Is there an “easy” fix for that or something I could do to flip them? If it would help I can provide you with some example data in the Analyze format.<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Thank you very much for all the work and afford you put into\r\nCardinal!</p>\r\n\r\n</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "mj8U4IxMDQAJ",
   "from": "kbemis",
   "author": "6033d3c0e7d242fa",
   "date": "2016-12-21T04:16:07Z",
   "subject": "Re: Analyze 7.5 data orientation",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Sven,<div><br></div><div>You can do:</div><div><br></div><div><p style=\"font-size:12px;line-height:normal;font-family:&#39;Andale Mono&#39;\"><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><font color=\"#000000\"></font></span></p><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><font color=\"#000000\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">coord</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">object</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> max</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">coord</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">object</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> coord</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">object</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span></div></code></font></div><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font><p></p>where <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">object</font> is your dataset. This will reverse the y-coordinates in your dataset.</div><div><br></div><div>If you wish to simply flip the image itself when it&#39;s plotted, you could instead do:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">image</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">object</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mz</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">798</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ylim</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rev</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">range</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">coord</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">object</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span></div></code></div><span style=\"font-family:monospace;color:rgb(102,102,0)\"><br></span></div><div>which only flips the plotting y-axis and leaves the coordinates in the dataset unchanged.</div><div><br></div><div>-Kylie</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
